course: dictionary
lesson: 13714
title: Instigator: the one who starts it
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An instigator is a person who starts something. Often it is trouble, like a fight, an argument or a protest, but it can also be a plan or an idea. «Who was the instigator of the food fight?» The clue is in the question: the instigator is the one who got it going.
explain: Look at the words around it. In «Maya was the instigator of the plan to build a den», the words «of the plan» and «to build» show that an instigator is the one who began the plan. Try your meaning in the sentence: «Maya was the one who started the plan» still makes sense, so you have it.
explain: Instigator is a noun, so it names a person. Phrases like «chief instigator» mean the main person who started something. In history lessons, instigators started revolts or protests, so when you read about them, ask who began the trouble.
fact: An instigator is a person who starts something.
fact: «Instigator» can mean a person who starts trouble, such as a fight, an argument or a protest.
fact: «Instigator» can also mean a person who causes a plan or idea to begin, as in «the instigator of the idea».
fact: Useful phrases: the instigator of, chief instigator.
fact: In history, instigators started revolts or protests.
exercise: «The teacher asked who was the instigator of the food fight.» What is an instigator? | *The person who started it | The person who cleaned up afterwards | The teacher who ate lunch early | The person who was hit by the food
exercise: «Ravi was the chief instigator of the plan to build a treehouse.» What does chief instigator mean? | A small, quiet helper | Someone who forgot the plan | Someone who helped clean up after the plan | *The main person who started the plan
exercise: In history lessons, who are instigators? | People who sang songs at school | *People who started revolts or protests | People who wrote the history books | People who visited the museum
test: Which word means the person who starts an argument or trouble? | Referee | Customer | *Instigator | Spectator
test: Which sentence uses instigator correctly? | *Lily was the instigator of the plan, because she suggested it first. | The rain was an instigator, so we ran inside. | He read an instigator in the library. | The cake was the instigator of the party.
exam: Tom says, «Nina was the instigator of the protest.» What does Tom mean? | Nina watched the protest from a window | Nina stopped the protest | *Nina started the protest | Nina cleaned up after the protest
